{
  "_": "APPROVED PAGE COMPOSITIONS — A PROPOSAL, awaiting the maintainer. Nothing here is wired into the shipping catalogue in docs/atlas/composition/src/, into the lesson, or into lesson-studio.html.",
  "theProblem": "Discrete slot spans were not enough. A pattern was still a collection of INDEPENDENTLY legal slots, so a six-column object could be legal at the left of a twelve-column row with nothing in the other six, and nothing in the system had an opinion about vertical structure at all. Measured on the shipped lesson: the portrait visual.explanation page paints its graph 683px wide at the left of a 1152px row, leaving 469px that no region owns — and 683px is not any span the grid names.",
  "theFix": "A PATTERN RESOLVES TO A NAMED COMPOSITION BLUEPRINT, not to a set of spans. A blueprint fixes the whole page: which rows exist, which columns each region takes, whether regions share a row or follow one another, how each row behaves vertically, and the named step between one semantic region and the next. A slot cannot independently decide \"six columns, left\".",
  "theChain": "lesson intent → pattern → approved composition blueprint → slots and rows → media geometry selects among the approved blueprints → content fills it. Never: content → measurements → slot sizes → hope the result looks balanced.",
  "theMostImportantRule": "NO ACTIVE ROW MAY CONTAIN UNEXPLAINED COLUMNS. A row holding one six-column media slot may not take columns 1-6 and call 7-12 margin. It must be centred within the row, accompanied by another semantic region, given a wider approved footprint, or moved into a different row composition. Reading margin is legal AROUND a coherent composition; it is not a licence for an unfinished half-row, and free columns beside MEDIA are never a margin, because media has no reading measure to be bound by.",
  "notAResolver": "Nothing here measures prose, counts steps or computes an occupancy fraction. A blueprint is chosen from categorical inputs — the surface, the media geometry class, or an authored form — and the set a pattern may choose from is written down. The program is not deciding whether a page looks balanced; it is choosing among a handful of pages a human already decided are finished.",
  "rhythm": {
    "_": "THE SECOND AXIS. The gap between two semantic regions is a NAMED STEP declared by the blueprint at that boundary, drawn from the 8px scale in grid.json. It is not the sum of whatever paddings the two regions happen to carry.",
    "steps": {
      "tight": 16,
      "normal": 32,
      "section": 56
    },
    "rule": "Every row but the last declares `gapAfter`. A conclusion takes `section`; regions within one movement take `normal`; parts of one region take `tight`."
  },
  "rowModes": {
    "_": "THE ROW CONTRACT. Width was never the whole story: the same 6/6 can be a finished comparison or a pair of columns with a cliff of white between them, and only the vertical rule tells them apart.",
    "hug": "The row is exactly as tall as its contents. No child stretches to match unrelated material. Ordinary worked examples, explanations and readings are all hug rows.",
    "paired": "Two sibling regions deliberately share a row and top-align on a declared origin. A paired row must name `align`, an `imbalanceMax` in px, and a `pairReason`. The atlas measures how far the two children actually end apart and fails the blueprint if it exceeds what the blueprint declared.",
    "workspace": "A deliberately substantial area — an interactive, a workbook page, a drawing surface — whose height is designed rather than derived. Only a workspace row may be materially taller than its prose."
  },
  "regionRoles": {
    "slot": "a named slot of the pattern.",
    "reading-margin": "free columns BESIDE A PROSE SLOT, because prose is bound by the reading measure and the page is wider. Never beside media. Never wider than the surface allows.",
    "containment": "the free columns either side of a CENTRED split, declared by the split and always symmetric."
  },
  "marginMax": {
    "desktop": 4,
    "tablet": 1,
    "phone": 0
  },
